+# Work around Qiskit/qiskit#17213: with qiskit 2.6.0, on Linux Python extensions
+# (such as qiskit._accelerate and pyscf's bundled libgomp) can exhaust glibc's
+# default static TLS surplus ("cannot allocate memory in static TLS block").
+#
+# glibc sizes the per-thread TLS block at startup and keeps only a small surplus for
+# libraries dlopen'ed afterwards that need initial-exec TLS. Every Python extension
+# module arrives that way, so they all draw on that surplus, and the one that fails is
+# whichever asks last rather than whichever took the space. qiskit 2.6.0's _accelerate
+# grew from 736 to 1544 bytes of it, leaving too little for subsequent extensions.
+# This affects notebooks (which also load Aer and sbd-eigensolver) and coverage (which
+# loads coverage's tracer extension before running the test suite), as well as any other
+# environment importing pyscf. The value matches the one qiskit-aer uses.
+#
+# Linux-only: this is a glibc mechanism and macOS has no equivalent limit. Note glibc
+# silently ignores an unrecognized tunable name, so a typo here would look like a
+# working config. Remove this once 17213 is fixed in a release.
+set_env =
+  linux: GLIBC_TUNABLES = glibc.rtld.optional_static_tls=4096
 extras =
   test
 commands =
